    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "Owner responses now include `telephoneDisplay`, a readable version of the stored number, and `telephone` is still returned as raw E.164. The full test suite passes (10 tests, 0 failures), including the acceptance test that expects `0412 345 678` to come back as `\"+61412345678\"` with a display of `\"+61 412 345 678\"`.\n\n**What changed:**\n- **`OwnerTelephone.format(e164)`** (in `rest/validation/OwnerTelephone.java`) builds the display value: `+`, the country code, a space, then the remaining digits in groups of three.\n  - To find where the country code ends, it uses the standard telephone country-code lengths: codes starting with 1 or 7 are one digit, a fixed list of codes are two digits, and all others are three.\n  - If the stored value isn't valid E.164 or is null, it's returned unchanged.\n- **`OwnerMapper`** fills `telephoneDisplay` through a new `toTelephoneDisplay(owner)` method, the same way the other calculated fields are set. That applies to every owner response, including the paged v2 list.\n- **`openapi.yml`** documents `telephoneDisplay` as a read-only field on `Owner`. The DTO is generated from this file.\n\n**One choice of mine:** strict groups of three would leave a single digit on its own at the end, as in `+1 608 555 265 4`. When that would happen, I join it to the group before it, so the US seed numbers show as `+1 608 555 2654`. Australian numbers (`+61`) are unaffected. The rule is written into the API description.",
